1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,864
This woman died in 1951,

2
00:00:01,864 --> 00:00:03,551
but her cells are still alive today,

3
00:00:03,551 --> 00:00:05,026
and they've even been to space.

4
00:00:05,026 --> 00:00:06,351
It's 1951.

5
00:00:06,351 --> 00:00:08,514
Henrietta Lacks is a thirty-year-old mother of

6
00:00:08,514 --> 00:00:10,251
five who grew up farming tobacco in

7
00:00:10,251 --> 00:00:10,923
Virginia.

8
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:12,335
She goes to Johns Hopkins,

9
00:00:12,335 --> 00:00:13,573
one of the few hospitals that will

10
00:00:13,573 --> 00:00:14,709
treat Black patients.

11
00:00:14,709 --> 00:00:16,384
Doctors find cervical cancer,

12
00:00:16,384 --> 00:00:17,321
and during treatment,

13
00:00:17,321 --> 00:00:18,509
they take a piece of her tumor

14
00:00:18,509 --> 00:00:19,429
without asking her.

15
00:00:19,429 --> 00:00:20,842
It lands in George Gey's lab,

16
00:00:20,842 --> 00:00:22,824
where human cells keep dying within days.

17
00:00:22,824 --> 00:00:23,361
Hers don't.

18
00:00:23,361 --> 00:00:24,436
They double about once a day,

19
00:00:24,436 --> 00:00:25,137
and never stop.

20
00:00:25,137 --> 00:00:25,512
You see,

21
00:00:25,512 --> 00:00:27,375
normal cells have a built-in countdown,

22
00:00:27,375 --> 00:00:28,587
but a virus in her tumor had

23
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:29,396
switched hers off.

24
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:30,684
Henrietta dies that October,

25
00:00:30,684 --> 00:00:31,359
at thirty-one.

26
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:32,550
Her cells keep going.

27
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,750
Scientists name them HeLa,

28
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:34,800
mail them around the world,

29
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,125
and use them to test the polio

30
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:36,800
vaccine,

31
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:37,575
fight cancer,

32
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:38,312
map our genes,

33
00:00:38,312 --> 00:00:40,181
and even send human cells into orbit.

34
00:00:40,181 --> 00:00:40,981
They show up in more than a

35
00:00:40,981 --> 00:00:42,286
hundred thousand studies.

36
00:00:42,286 --> 00:00:42,649
Luckily,

37
00:00:42,649 --> 00:00:44,261
her cells helped protect millions.

38
00:00:44,261 --> 00:00:44,861
Unluckily,

39
00:00:44,861 --> 00:00:45,993
nobody ever asked her.

40
00:00:45,993 --> 00:00:47,693
Her family only learns her cells are

41
00:00:47,693 --> 00:00:49,418
alive more than twenty years later,

42
00:00:49,418 --> 00:00:50,156
and for decades,

43
00:00:50,156 --> 00:00:51,205
they don't see a cent.

44
00:00:51,205 --> 00:00:52,280
In 2023,

45
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:53,805
they finally settle with a company that

46
00:00:53,805 --> 00:00:54,714
sold her cells.

47
00:00:54,714 --> 00:00:55,314
So right now,

48
00:00:55,314 --> 00:00:56,152
somewhere in a lab,

49
00:00:56,152 --> 00:00:57,864
a piece of Henrietta is still dividing,

50
00:00:57,864 --> 00:00:58,552
and this time,

51
00:00:58,552 --> 00:01:00,089
the world knows her name.
